A||||4||burdell1||buffing out spots
Z||000000||burdell1||08-29-99||01:04 PM||||My 85 Fiero GT is getting a few spots where it looks like something has been tarnishing the clearcoat, but it doesn't look that serious. However, when I try to buff it out (usually with Colorback or wax) it doesn't get better. A friend of mine has an electric buffer, but I wasn't sure what kind of stuff to use with it that would do a really good job. Any suggestions?<br>
Z||000001||Bruce||08-29-99||04:11 PM||||I used Meguiar's Marine System Step 1 (cleaner). It worked really well on some spots I had accumulated on the front and rear deck. You just wax afterwards. It's a mild abrasive, so you may want to test it first.
Z||000002||Cooter||08-29-99||07:59 PM||||After you buff the spots, look at the buffing pad to see if it has got the car's color on it. If it does, your clear coat is gone and a repaint will be in your future.
Z||000003||Bruce||08-29-99||08:32 PM||||I just had a little dirt accumulate on my wax pad, so it worked for me. The key is to use a high quality wax after you remove the spots.
Z||000004||ChadMan||08-30-99||07:55 AM||||The two best waxes I've used with my electric buffer are Meguiar's (9 bucks a bottle) and believe it or not, good old Turtle Wax (3 bucks a bottle).  Meguiar's lasts longer.<p>Be sure to use a liquid wax or you'll get swirls.  And don't put on too heavy a coat or you'll wind up having to rub it out by hand anyway, which defeats the purpose of a buffer!  (Yes, I've done this.)
